---
import Footer from "../../components/Footer.astro";
import Nav from "../../components/Nav.astro";
import SiteLayout from "../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";

const title = "Docs: Dream Score & Code Flow — Harness Dreams";
const description =
  "A practical guide to the Harness Dreams Dream Score, code-Code Flow graph, tracked signals, and advice rules.";
---

<SiteLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <Nav />
  <main id="main">
    <article class="article-page">
      <header class="article-hero">
        <div class="container section-narrow">
          <span class="eyebrow">Docs / Dream Score</span>
          <h1>From local transcripts to one comparable score.</h1>
          <p class="lead">
            The tester MVP turns Claude Code and Codex history into a Dream
            Score, code-Code Flow graph, braggable counters, quotes, trends,
            highlights, and a few evidence-anchored advice cards.
          </p>
        </div>
      </header>

      <div class="container article-grid">
        <aside class="article-aside card">
          <img
            src="/story-end-of-day.png"
            alt=""
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            class="aside-image"
          />
          <span class="mono">At a glance</span>
          <strong>4 components</strong>
          <p>Alignment, effectiveness, efficiency, and rhythm combine into the Dream Score.</p>
        </aside>

        <div class="article-body">
          <h2>1. Score</h2>
          <p>
            The Dream Score is a deterministic 0-100 measure of collaboration
            health. Alignment asks whether the agent understood you.
            Effectiveness asks whether the work landed. Efficiency asks whether
            context and retries were wasteful. Rhythm asks whether the day had
            sustainable focus and breaks.
          </p>

          <h2>2. Track signals</h2>
          <p>
            The app reads local session events: turns, timestamps, harness and
            project attribution, tool outcomes, model calls, token usage,
            cache usage, frustration hits, hedge hits, and Codex token
            estimates. Missing data is marked honestly instead of filled with
            fake precision.
          </p>

          <h2>3. Draw the code-Code Flow graph</h2>
          <p>
            Every five-minute bucket becomes Deep Focus, Rapid Exchange, Agent
            Running, Light Touch, or Rest. Markers call out harness switches,
            frustration clusters, happy moments, peak parallel sessions, and
            the longest autonomous agent run.
          </p>

          <h2>4. Explain trends and highlights</h2>
          <p>
            Trends compare the last 14 active days against a 60-day baseline.
            Highlights catch records, week-over-week changes, best days, and
            productive windows. Both layers are template sentences over
            computed values — no LLM decides what is true.
          </p>

          <h2>5. Keep advice ambient</h2>
          <p>
            Coach cards are capped at three and must link to visible evidence.
            They can suggest a prompt habit, tool check, or copyable snippet,
            but the MVP never applies config changes, opens branches, or edits
            project files.
          </p>

          <h2>Score bands</h2>
          <p>
            90-100 is Lucid, 75-89 is Dreaming, 60-74 is Restless, and below 60
            is Turbulent. Rest days are rest, not zeroes; sparse days show a
            warming-up state until there is enough signal.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</SiteLayout>
